diff --git a/unit_tests/SpaceCharge/README.md b/unit_tests/SpaceCharge/README.md
index ef1bc34a4..37d1df872 100644
--- a/unit_tests/SpaceCharge/README.md
+++ b/unit_tests/SpaceCharge/README.md
@@ -73,3 +73,28 @@ serial solver for sizes 1, 8, 32 and 64. They cover neutral sources, incomplete
 batches, anisotropic rectangular grids, grid/spacing changes and the complete
 solve under both open and closed longitudinal boundary modes. CLI parsing,
 normalization and the same 100-step tracking pilot are checked separately.
+
+### FFTW threads within each slice transform
+
+`--2D5FFTThreads n` sets the FFTW planner's maximum thread count for FFT2D5
+(default 1), independently of `OMP_NUM_THREADS`, which controls the surrounding
+Kokkos kernels. For example, `OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 opalx case.in --2D5BatchSize 8
+--2D5FFTThreads 4` keeps Kokkos at four threads and permits FFTW plans to use
+up to four. FFTW may choose fewer threads for a small transform.
+
+OPALX initializes double-precision host FFTW thread support once before input
+processing. With OpenMP, its supported FFTW work-loop callback uses an explicit
+`num_threads(job_count)` clause. The built-in FFTW OpenMP loop otherwise uses
+ambient `OMP_NUM_THREADS`, which would cap this study at four executing workers
+even when the planner limit is larger. The callback changes no ambient OpenMP
+setting and is installed once for the process's double-precision FFTW plans. Each legacy/Green or native batched plan is constructed with the
+requested setting. Both transform directions are warmed within that scope because
+heFFTe creates FFTW plans lazily on execution. The previous process-wide setting is restored even
+if construction throws. Existing plans retain their own settings. No global
+OpenMP team-size change or IPPL modification is made. Plan creation remains
+serialized; cleanup is not called while other FFTW plans may still exist.
+CMake probes the linked FFTW interface for thread support. Serial-only FFTW
+installations retain the single-thread path; requesting more than one thread
+requires a thread-enabled host FFTW backend. The numerical test
+compares potentials/halos and fields for limits 1, 4 and 16, and verifies that
+OPALX leaves a caller's independent planner setting unchanged.
